<!-- Accordion Item 1 -->
<details class="group p-5 cursor-pointer" open="">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-headline-sm text-headline-sm font-bold text-on-surface list-none">
<span>How does EverydayKit handle floating-point precision discrepancies?</span>
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-outline group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">expand_more</span>
</summary>
<div class="pt-3 font-body-md text-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            Computers natively use base-2 floating-point representation (IEEE 754), which cannot represent certain base-10 fractions (such as 0.1 or 0.7) with infinite accuracy. EverydayKit converts values through intermediate rational fractions (numerator/denominator pairs) and applies an epsilon guard of 1e-15 before output formatting, preventing phantom decimals like 4,101.049900000001.
          </div>
</details>
<!-- Accordion Item 2 -->
<details class="group p-5 cursor-pointer">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-headline-sm text-headline-sm font-bold text-on-surface list-none">
<span>What is the difference between international and US survey feet?</span>
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-outline group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">expand_more</span>
</summary>
<div class="pt-3 font-body-md text-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            Since the 1959 international agreement, 1 international foot is legally defined as exactly 0.3048 meters. The historical US Survey Foot was defined as 1200/3937 meters (~0.30480061 m). On December 31, 2022, the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) officially superseded and retired the US Survey Foot in favor of the international foot definition, which this converter uses exclusively.
          </div>
</details>
<!-- Accordion Item 3 -->
<details class="group p-5 cursor-pointer">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-headline-sm text-headline-sm font-bold text-on-surface list-none">
<span>Are temperature conversions computed linearly or with absolute offsets?</span>
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-outline group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">expand_more</span>
</summary>
<div class="pt-3 font-body-md text-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            Unlike length or mass, temperature scales feature non-zero zero-points. EverydayKit uses affine transformations rather than simple scale factors: for Celsius to Fahrenheit, it applies \(T_F = T_C \times \frac{9}{5} + 32\); for Kelvin transitions, it employs the exact thermodynamic offset 273.15 K.
          </div>
</details>
<!-- Accordion Item 4 -->
<details class="group p-5 cursor-pointer">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-headline-sm text-headline-sm font-bold text-on-surface list-none">
<span>Can I use scientific notation for astronomical or subatomic measurements?</span>
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-outline group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">expand_more</span>
</summary>
<div class="pt-3 font-body-md text-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            Yes. EverydayKit seamlessly parses standard exponential notation formats (e.g. <code>1.496e11</code> for an Astronomical Unit in meters, or <code>6.674e-11</code> for gravitational metrics). When the scientific toggle is selected, output values are rendered in normalized standard mantissa and exponent formatting.
          </div>
</details>
